## Section One: Conservation of Loss Law

### 1.1 The Fundamental Equation

For a particle moving with a random step in a d-dimensional space, the probability density follows:

$$P(r, t) = \frac{1}{(4\pi D t)^{d/2}} \exp\left(-\frac{r^2}{4Dt}\right)$$

### 1.2 The Crucial Mathematical Conclusion

**Theorem (Conservation of Loss Law):**

As t → ∞:

$$\lim_{t \to \infty} P(0, t) = \begin{cases} 0 & \text{if } d \geq 3 \text{ (inevitable loss)} \\ 0 & \text{if } d = 2 \text{ (relative slowness)} \\ \infty & \text{if } d = 1 \text{ (confinement)} \end{cases}$$

**Physical Implication:** In higher dimensions (d ≥ 3), the particle "gets lost" in space with probability approaching 1.

### 1.3 Metric Loss Measure

We define the loss coefficient L:

$$L(d) = \lim_{t \to \infty} \frac{\ln P_{return}(t)}{\ln t} = -\frac{d-2}{2}$$

| Dimension d | L | Behavior |
|-------------|-------|----------------|
| 1 | +0.5 | Confinement |
| 2 | 0 | Recurrent |
| 3 | -0.5 | Transient |
| ∞ | -∞ | Immediate Loss |

