## Section Two: Angular Symmetry Breaking

### 2.1 k-Angle Model

In the two-dimensional space (d=2), we reduce higher dimensions through k equal angles:

$$\theta_n = \frac{2\pi n}{k}, \quad n = 0, 1, \ldots, k-1$$

Unit vectors:

$$\mathbf{e}_n = (\cos\theta_n, \sin\theta_n)$$

### 2.2 Condition for Complete Symmetry

**Theorem:** The system preserves the "loss state" if and only if:

$$\sum_{n=0}^{k-1} p_n \mathbf{e}_n = \mathbf{0} \quad \text{and} \quad p_n = \frac{1}{k} \quad \forall n$$

### 2.3 Symmetry Breaking Coefficient

$$\boxed{\delta_\theta = \left| \sum_{n=0}^{k-1} p_n e^{i\theta_n} \right| = |P_{complex}|}$$

**Range:**
- δθ = 0: Complete symmetry (preserved loss)
- δθ = 1: Complete breaking (determined motion)

